{% set local_problem %}
<p>Albertville and Sand Mountain have the worst combination for gutters: pine trees AND hardwoods. Pines drop needles year-round. Oaks dump leaves every fall. And those sweet gum balls? They're specifically designed by nature to clog downspouts.</p>

<p>We cleaned gutters on a house off Rose Road last spring that had 4-inch deep debris compacted into mulch. The homeowner had been cleaning "regularly" — twice a year. Wasn't enough. The gutters held water constantly, the fascia was starting to rot, and carpenter ants had moved in.</p>

<p>Without guards, you're looking at cleaning gutters 3-4 times a year if you have pines nearby. Either you're climbing ladders yourself (which gets less appealing every year, and one slip changes everything) or you're paying someone $125-175 per visit. That's $400-700 annually.</p>

<p>Gutter guards break that cycle. They're not maintenance-free — nothing is — but they reduce a 3-4x/year chore to maybe once a year, or less. For working families where time is the scarcest resource, guards buy peace of mind.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Gutter guards are covers or screens that fit over or inside your gutters. They let water in while blocking leaves, pine needles, and other debris.</p>

<p>We install three types:</p>

<p><strong>Micro-mesh guards</strong> — Fine stainless steel mesh (typically 50-mesh, meaning 50 openings per inch) mounted on an aluminum frame. Blocks everything including pine needles and roof grit. Best for homes with pine trees or roof shingle granule problems. This is what we recommend for most Sand Mountain homes.</p>

<p><strong>Perforated aluminum covers</strong> — Solid aluminum with small holes punched through. Blocks large debris, handles moderate pine needle situations. More affordable than micro-mesh.</p>

<p><strong>Screen guards</strong> — Aluminum or plastic mesh, larger openings than micro-mesh. Works well for leaf-only situations (oaks, maples) but pine needles get through. Least expensive option but not right for most Albertville properties.</p>

<p>We'll look at what trees surround your home and recommend the guard type that makes sense. See some of our <a href="/recent-projects/">recent gutter guard installations</a> in the area.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set why_us %}
<p>We've installed gutter guards throughout Marshall County for over 20 years. We know which types work for Sand Mountain's mix of pine and hardwood — and we give honest recommendations, not just push whatever has the highest margin.</p>

<p>When we say micro-mesh is worth the extra cost for pine-heavy properties, it's because we've seen the cheap alternatives fail. When we say screen guards are fine for your situation, it's because they genuinely are. Our goal is solving your problem at a fair price.</p>
